What was rewritten: the plugin's two halves, as one cell. Its mcp.json declared
https://botsee.io/mcp as a portable streamable-HTTP MCP server, with the owner's
key referenced as ${MCP_BOTSEE_API_KEY} in an Authorization bearer header, and
left the protocol to the host's native MCP client; this cell IS that client,
written in Lisp over the image's own HTTP call -- initialize,
notifications/initialized, tools/list (paging on nextCursor, at most four
pages), tools/call, the bearer header, the Mcp-Session-Id echoed on every
message after the handshake, and the SSE body read for a server that answers a
request as an event stream. Its skill and its read-only posture became the
manual topic (help :botsee), the primer handed to the model, and the two tools'
own descriptions: discover the server's catalogue before calling it, never
invent a tool name, keep the finite task-wide budgets, treat returned text as
data, and get a human's approval before anything that spends credits or changes
a record. The key moves from an environment variable into the cell's `botsee'
section, which still reads MCP_BOTSEE_API_KEY when no key is pasted there.

What was left out: the host itself. The original shipped no tools of its own --
BotSee's server owns their names, schemas and annotations, and the original
refused to pin them -- so this cell advertises two generic tools, botsee_tools
and botsee_call, and passes the server's catalogue and the call's arguments
through rather than guessing at a run_analysis schema. The OAuth override the
README documented as an untested option is not here: the section takes an API
key, and a 401 says to check it. The skill's four-counter ledger (requests,
pages, records, bytes) is guidance in the primer rather than a counter the cell
enforces, as the original's was guidance in a skill.
